\chapter{Restrictions and Acknowledgements}
\label{ch:reference}

\section{Restrictions}

weft is meant to stay simple, so a few restrictions are deliberate. Some
may be lifted over time; others seem fundamental.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Error handling is not uniform.} In some cases weft warns and
continues; in others it halts. This should be regularised.
\item \textbf{Undefined fragments warn but do not halt.} A reference to a
fragment that is never defined is written into the output surrounded by
\verb|<>|, which is convenient during development but may change.
\item \textbf{No \texttt{@@} in file names or index entries.}
\item \textbf{Fragment names are (almost) any \TeX.} You may switch fonts
or use math mode in a name, but keep braces, brackets, and dollar signs
balanced.
\item \textbf{Scraps hold anything}, but very long scraps (wide or tall)
may not typeset well. A long scrap can be allowed to break across a page
if defined with \verb|@@O|/\verb|@@D| instead of \verb|@@o|/\verb|@@d|;
this is a poor default (too many short scraps would break) but a useful
per-scrap choice.
\item \textbf{Output files share the current directory} with the
temporary files weft writes for its change-test, so all outputs should be
on the same file system --- or use \verb|-p| to redirect them.
\item \textbf{\LaTeX\ needs two passes.} Page-based scrap numbers are not
known until \LaTeX\ has run, so weft must be rerun afterwards for a clean
document (much as \LaTeX\ itself is rerun for an up-to-date table of
contents). weft warns when this is needed; otherwise \LaTeX\ warns that
labels may have changed. This does not apply to Markdown output, which
numbers sequentially and needs no extra passes.
\end{itemize}

\section{Acknowledgements}

weft is a fork of nuweb, which was written by Preston Briggs and
maintained over the years by Marc Mengel, Simon Wright, and Keith
Harwood. It in turn drew its name and several features from Norman
Ramsey's noweb, and stands on the foundation Donald Knuth laid with
\verb|WEB|.

Many people contributed time, ideas, and debugging to nuweb, among them
Osman Buyukisik, Manuel Carriba, Adrian Clarke, Tim Harvey, Michael
Lewis, Walter Ravenek, Rob Shillingsburg, Kayvan Sylvan, Dominique
de~Waleffe, and Scott Warren --- and George Greenwade, without whose
efforts most of them would never have heard of the tool. Later online
contributions came from Walter Brown, Nicky van~Foreest, Javier Goizueta,
and Alan Karp.

weft carries that work forward with a modernized, concept-organized
codebase, Markdown weave output, source-mapped section markers, and the
introspection toolkit of Chapter~\ref{ch:ai} --- while keeping the
simplicity that made nuweb worth forking.
